<section class="method-section reveal" id="how-it-works">
  <div class="method-intro">
    <div class="eyebrow">Offset Mechanics</div>
    <h2>Understanding EAT Offset and DST Rules</h2>
    <p>
      To convert Greenwich Mean Time to Eastern Africa Time, you must add 3 hours to the standard reference clock. The formula is written as follows:
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>EAT = GMT + 3 Hours</code>
    </p>
    <p>
      Eastern Africa is situated close to the equator. Because daylight hours remain nearly constant at roughly 12 hours year-round, there is no economic or energy benefit to shifting clocks. As a result, East African nations operate on a permanent offset of UTC+3 and do not observe daylight saving.
    </p>
    <p>
      This year-round consistency provides several benefits for global scheduling, remote SaaS companies, and international shipping logistics, as the local clock offset never changes.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="method-grid">
    <div class="method-card">
      <h3>Greenwich Mean Time</h3>
      <code>UTC+0 Baseline</code>
      <p>GMT is the solar time standard centered on the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, London. It does not observe daylight saving shifts.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="method-card">
      <h3>Eastern Africa Time</h3>
      <code>UTC+3 Offset</code>
      <p>EAT is active year-round. It operates exactly 3 hours ahead of GMT, providing constant timezone synchronization.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="method-card">
      <h3>Daylight Saving</h3>
      <code>None Observed</code>
      <p>Because EAT is located close to the equator, it never transitions to daylight saving time, remaining UTC+3 constantly.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

