<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     COMMON CONFUSIONS
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="source-notes" class="source-notes reveal">
  <h2>Common Confusions: EAT, EEST, CAT, and UTC+3</h2>
  <p>
    Timezone abbreviations can be confusing. Let's clarify the key definitions and differences:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>EAT vs. EEST</strong>: Eastern Africa Time (EAT) and Eastern European Summer Time (EEST) both operate at UTC+3. However, EEST is a daylight saving zone used only in summer by countries like Greece and Ukraine. EAT is active year-round in East Africa.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>EAT vs. CAT</strong>: Eastern Africa Time (EAT, UTC+3) runs 1 hour ahead of Central Africa Time (CAT, UTC+2). CAT is observed in countries like Egypt, South Africa, and Zambia.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>GMT vs. UTC</strong>: GMT is a solar time standard, while UTC is the modern atomic standard. For general scheduling and conversion, their offsets are identical (+0).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Arabia Standard Time (AST)</strong>: AST is also UTC+3 and runs year-round, matching EAT's offset. However, it applies to Middle Eastern nations such as Saudi Arabia and Iraq rather than African countries.
    </li>
  </ul>

